2. GENERATOR STATOR RESISTANCE - THE ohm vs pu QUESTION (WB U3 = "0.00089 ohm")
==============================================================================
Reading A (TEXT OF THE CELL, mandated): Ra = 0.00089 ohm
   Ra_pu(458 MVA) = Ra_ohm/Zbase = 0.00089000 / 1.05676856 = 8.42190083e-04 pu
   Ra_pu(100 MVA) = 1.83884298e-04 pu
Reading B (REJECTED): Ra = 0.00089 pu  ->  9.40524017e-04 ohm
Numerical separation between the two readings = 5.6769 % (Zbase is close to 1 ohm)
  => the ohm/pu error is SMALL in effect here; it is corrected on evidence, not magnitude.

Independent physical test - armature time constant Ta = X2/(w*Ra):
  Ta from reading A (ohm) = 0.847375 s      (workbook AG3 states 0.704 s)
  Ta from reading B (pu)  = 0.801855 s
  Ra required to reproduce Ta = 0.704 s exactly: 1.01370847e-03 pu  (= 0.00107126 ohm)
  ratio to reading A = 1.20366 ; ratio to reading B = 1.13900
  IEC copper correction 20 degC -> 75 degC = (235+75)/(235+20) = 1.21569
  |A - kcu|/kcu = 0.989 %   |B - kcu|/kcu = 6.308 %
  => Ta is consistent with reading A being the COLD (20 degC) stator resistance
     and Ta being quoted at 75 degC. Supporting evidence, not proof; the proof
     is the literal unit in the cell.

==============================================================================
3. SCR CROSS-CHECK - DOES THE WORKBOOK ITSELF IDENTIFY 1.663 AS SATURATED Xd?
==============================================================================
Definition: SCR = 1/Xd(saturated).
  1/SCR   = 1/0.601 = 1.663894
  SIE 2.1.1 "Synchronous reactance (sat.)" xd = 1.663 pu
  |1/SCR - 1.663|/1.663 = 0.0537 %
  1/1.663 = 0.601323  -> rounds to 0.601 = workbook SCR
  And 1/Xd(unsat 1.783) = 0.560852, which does NOT equal the stated SCR.
  => The workbook's OWN SCR column independently identifies 1.663 as the
     saturated Xd of the same machine. 1.783 vs 1.663 is a saturation-basis
     distinction, NOT a source conflict.

